        <section class="page-heading">
            <h2>Tips & Tricks</h2>

            <p>
                Smart landscaping does not have to mean expensive landscaping.
                A little planning can save money and create better results. As plants grow and spread, they can be divided and moved to fill gaps or create new arrangements.
            </p>
        </section>


        <section class="tips-container">

            <article class="tip">
                <h3>1. Start with a plan</h3>

                <p>
                    One of the most expensive DIY mistakes is buying plants
                    before creating a plan. Measure your yard and sketch where
                    major features such as patios, paths, trees, and garden
                    beds will go before shopping. Take your time; it doesn't all have to be planted out at once.
                </p>
            </article>


            <article class="tip">
                <h3>2. Buy smaller plants</h3>

                <p>
                    Large plants provide instant impact, but smaller plants are
                    usually much less expensive. Many shrubs and perennials
                    grow quickly once established, making smaller plants a
                    smart choice for budget-conscious gardeners.
                </p>
            </article>


            <article class="tip">
                <h3>3. Repeat plants</h3>

                <p>
                    Avoid buying one of every plant that catches your eye.
                    Repeating the same plants throughout a landscape creates
                    rhythm and makes the design feel intentional.
                </p>
            </article>


            <article class="tip">
                <h3>4. Think about mature size</h3>

                <p>
                    That tiny shrub at the garden center may eventually become
                    six feet wide. Check the mature height and width before
                    planting so you do not have to move or remove plants later.
                </p>
            </article>


            <article class="tip">
                <h3>5. Invest in structure first</h3>

                <p>
                    Paths, trees, patios, hedges, and larger shrubs form the
                    structure of a landscape. Establish those elements first,
                    then add decorative plants and accessories over time.
                </p>
            </article>


            <article class="tip">
                <h3>6. Divide the project into phases</h3>

                <p>
                    You do not need to finish your entire yard in one summer.
                    Completing one well-planned section at a time can make a
                    large project much more affordable and manageable. Remeber to enjoy the process.
                </p>
            </article>

        </section>

    </main>


